{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli who ruled from 1152 until his death in 1187 He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Tripoli and a member of the House of Poitiers-Toulouse The Crusader state of Tripoli was established in 1102 following the First Crusade and was one of the four main Crusader states in the Levant along with the Kingdom of Jerusalem the County of Edessa and the Principality of Antioch\n\nRaymond III was still a child when his father was killed in 1152 and he inherited the county under the regency of his mother Hodierna He came of age in 1164 and married Eschiva of Saint Omer the daughter of William of Saint Omer the constable of Tripoli Raymond and Eschiva had four children including Bohemond III who would succeed his father as Count of Tripoli\n\nRaymond III was an important figure in the politics of the Crusader states He allied himself with the Byzantine Empire against the principality of Antioch and played a key role in the defense of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the Second Crusade He also participated in the military campaigns of the Kingdom of Jerusalem against the Zengids and the Ayyubids\n\n*Relations with the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\n----------------------------------------\n\nRaymond III had a complex relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem He was a vassal of the King of Jerusalem but also a rival for power In 1156 Raymond was accused of conspiring against King Baldwin III of Jerusalem and was exiled to Antioch He returned to Tripoli in 1157 but was exiled again in 1160 after supporting the claim of the Count of Jaffa to the throne of Jerusalem over that of Amalric I Raymond returned to Tripoli in 1162 and maintained a tense relationship with Amalric I until the latter's death in 1174\n\nRaymond III supported Amalric I's successor Baldwin IV against the claims of Raymond of Antioch and Philip of Flanders In 1179 Raymond and Baldwin IV defeated a Zengid army at the Battle of Marj Ayyun Raymond also supported Baldwin IV's efforts to maintain the succession of the Leper King by supporting the claim of Baldwin V over that of Sibylla and her husband Guy of Lusignan\n\n*Relations with the Byzantine Empire*\n---------------------------------------\n\nRaymond III had a close relationship with the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus He allied himself with Manuel against the Principality of Antioch in 1158 and again in 1176 He also supported Manuel's efforts to assert his authority over the Crusader states and to establish a Byzantine presence in the Levant\n\nRaymond III's alliance with the Byzantine Empire was not always popular with his fellow Crusaders He was accused of betraying the interests of the Crusader states by allying himself with the Byzantines and was criticized for his close relationship with Manuel\n\n*Defense of Tripoli*\n--------------------\n\nRaymond III faced numerous threats to the County of Tripoli during his reign He defended the county against attacks by the Zengids and the Ayyubids He also had to deal with internal threats to his rule including a rebellion by the Order of the Temple in 1157 and a dispute over the succession to the County of Edessa in 1159\n\nIn 1187 Raymond III was killed during the Battle of Hattin while fighting against the Ayyubid Sultan Saladin The defeat of the Crusader army at Hattin marked the beginning of the end of the Crusader presence in the Levant The County of Tripoli would continue to exist until 1289 but its power and influence were greatly diminished after the death of Raymond III.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -3.9219088648678735e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001627074379939586}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -1.2544739246368408}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -2.4676019165781327e-05}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -2.145764938177308e-06}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", 